# The US Geological Survey publishes earthquakes worldwide through the standard FDSN event web
# service, run by the National Earthquake Information Center in Golden, Colorado. These fields are
# written by hand and search the box around Colorado unless a query gives a point or a box.

extend type Query {
	"""
	Earthquakes recorded by the US Geological Survey (USGS), in Colorado unless the query gives another area, with
	their magnitude, place, time and depth, one page at a time. The USGS National Earthquake Information Center in
	Golden, Colorado, runs the catalogue. Use it to answer whether the ground shook near Denver or anywhere in
	Colorado, and how strong the latest earthquakes were.
	Without a point or a box, the field searches the box around Colorado, from latitude 36.99 to 41.0 and longitude
	-109.06 to -102.04. Without a startTime, it searches the last 30 days. The box around Colorado had 145
	earthquakes in 2025 and 221 in 2024, the strongest of magnitude 3.8 since 2024, and a search within 100 km of
	downtown Denver found 3 since the start of 2024.
	The catalogue can take 10 to 40 seconds to answer a search it has not seen before. Selecting totalEarthquakes or
	totalPages costs a second call, so leave them out unless the question needs them.
	"""
	usgsEarthquakes(
		"The first day to search, for example 2026-01-01. Leave it out for the last 30 days."
		startTime: Date
		"The last day to search, included. Leave it out to search until now."
		endTime: Date
		"The smallest magnitude to return, for example 2.5. Leave it out for every magnitude."
		minMagnitude: Float
		"The latitude of a point to search around, for example 39.7392 for downtown Denver. Give longitude as well."
		latitude: Float
		"The longitude of the point, negative in Colorado, for example -104.9903 for downtown Denver."
		longitude: Float
		"How far from the point to search, in kilometres. Leave it out for 100 km."
		maxRadiusKm: Float
		"A box of latitudes and longitudes to search in, in place of a point. Leave both out for the box around Colorado."
		box: UsgsBox
		"The order of the earthquakes. Leave it out for the most recent first."
		orderBy: UsgsEarthquakeOrder = TIME
		"The page to return, starting at 1. A value below 1 returns the first page."
		page: Int! = 1
		"The number of earthquakes on a page, from 1 to 50. A value outside that range is moved to the nearest limit."
		size: Int! = 10
	): UsgsEarthquakePage!

	"""
	The number of earthquakes in the US Geological Survey catalogue that match the filters, in Colorado unless the
	query gives another area, in one small call. Use it for questions such as how many earthquakes Colorado had this
	year. The filters work as for usgsEarthquakes.
	"""
	usgsEarthquakeCount(
		"The first day to search, for example 2026-01-01. Leave it out for the last 30 days."
		startTime: Date
		"The last day to search, included. Leave it out to search until now."
		endTime: Date
		"The smallest magnitude to count, for example 2.5. Leave it out for every magnitude."
		minMagnitude: Float
		"The latitude of a point to search around. Give longitude as well."
		latitude: Float
		"The longitude of the point, negative in Colorado."
		longitude: Float
		"How far from the point to search, in kilometres. Leave it out for 100 km."
		maxRadiusKm: Float
		"A box of latitudes and longitudes to search in, in place of a point. Leave both out for the box around Colorado."
		box: UsgsBox
	): Int!
}

"""
A box of latitudes and longitudes for the USGS earthquake search. Colorado's box runs from latitude 36.99 to 41.0
and from longitude -109.06 to -102.04.
"""
input UsgsBox {
	"The southern edge, for example 36.99 for Colorado."
	minLatitude: Float!
	"The northern edge, for example 41.0 for Colorado."
	maxLatitude: Float!
	"The western edge, for example -109.06 for Colorado."
	minLongitude: Float!
	"The eastern edge, for example -102.04 for Colorado."
	maxLongitude: Float!
}

"The orders of the USGS earthquake search."
enum UsgsEarthquakeOrder {
	"The most recent first."
	TIME
	"The strongest first."
	MAGNITUDE
}

"One page of earthquakes from the USGS catalogue."
type UsgsEarthquakePage {
	"The earthquakes on this page."
	earthquakes: [UsgsEarthquake!]!
	"The page that was returned, starting at 1."
	page: Int!
	"The number of pages available for the same filters and size."
	totalPages: Int!
	"The number of earthquakes that match the filters, counted across every page."
	totalEarthquakes: Int!
}

"An earthquake or another seismic event from the US Geological Survey catalogue."
type UsgsEarthquake {
	"The id of the event, for example us7000thsk."
	id: ID!
	"The magnitude and the place, for example M 1.8 - 28 km WSW of Yampa, Colorado."
	title: String
	magnitude: Float
	"How the magnitude was measured, for example ml for local magnitude."
	magnitudeType: String
	"The place, described by its distance from a town, for example 28 km WSW of Yampa, Colorado."
	place: String
	"When the earthquake happened, in UTC in ISO-8601, for example 2026-09-16T06:46:53.706Z."
	time: String
	"When the USGS last updated the event, in UTC in ISO-8601."
	updated: String
	"The latitude of the epicentre."
	latitude: Float
	"The longitude of the epicentre."
	longitude: Float
	"The depth of the focus below the surface, in kilometres."
	depthInKilometres: Float
	"The kind of event, for example earthquake or mining explosion."
	type: String
	"Whether a seismologist has reviewed the event: automatic or reviewed."
	status: String
	"How significant the event is, from 0 to about 1,000, from its magnitude, felt reports and estimated impact."
	significance: Int
	"The number of people who told the USGS that they felt it. Null until someone reports feeling it."
	feltReports: Int
	"The alert level of the USGS PAGER system for shaking damage, for example green. Null for the small events of Colorado."
	alert: String
	"The page of the event on the USGS website."
	url: String
}
